WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
In middle-aged and elderly organ donors, much of the healthy oesophageal lining

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.800
carried a mutation in one cancer gene, NOTCH1.

3
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.920
How does one mutant cell's family come to fill a lining that is replaced all the

4
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:15.920
time?

5
00:00:18.683 --> 00:00:21.083
Experiments in mouse oesophagus show how.

6
00:00:22.303 --> 00:00:23.943
Here is the lining, cut across.

7
00:00:25.323 --> 00:00:27.183
Only the bottom row of cells divides.

8
00:00:28.603 --> 00:00:32.563
That row is the basal layer, and its dividing cells are progenitors.

9
00:00:33.683 --> 00:00:38.283
Each daughter of a progenitor either stays in the bottom row or leaves it.

10
00:00:39.663 --> 00:00:43.283
Cells that leave differentiate, ride upward and are shed from the top.

11
00:00:44.563 --> 00:00:49.623
So the lining keeps its cell number steady only if, on average, it makes as many

12
00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:50.643
cells as it loses.

13
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:53.773
Watch one progenitor divide.

14
00:00:55.173 --> 00:00:57.373
Each division has three possible outcomes.

15
00:00:58.693 --> 00:01:00.833
Both daughters stay: call that PP.

16
00:01:01.893 --> 00:01:04.193
One stays and one leaves: PD.

17
00:01:05.053 --> 00:01:06.973
Or both leave: DD.

18
00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:10.657
In normal lining, these outcomes are balanced.

19
00:01:12.017 --> 00:01:13.657
PP comes up as often as DD.

20
00:01:14.977 --> 00:01:18.477
So on average, each division leaves one progenitor behind.

21
00:01:19.857 --> 00:01:22.677
One in, one out, and the bottom row holds steady.

22
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.360
Now one basal cell loses Notch1, and we mark it gold.

23
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.620
Every cell descended from it inherits the gold mark.

24
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.540
That family of cells is a clone.

25
00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:38.943
Its clock runs no faster than its blue neighbours' clocks.

26
00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:42.243
The mouse data point to one difference.

27
00:01:43.543 --> 00:01:46.583
Its divisions give PP a little more often than DD.

28
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:50.273
Before we run the clock forward, a question.

29
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:56.073
On average, will the gold clone hold steady, die out, or spread along the row?

30
00:02:01.717 --> 00:02:03.237
On average, it spreads.

31
00:02:04.637 --> 00:02:09.177
In this simplest picture, each division leaves a little more than one progenitor on

32
00:02:09.177 --> 00:02:13.697
average. Round after round, that small tilt compounds.

33
00:02:15.237 --> 00:02:16.257
Now watch the edges.

34
00:02:17.757 --> 00:02:20.157
Blue neighbours there differentiate and lift away.

35
00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:25.367
Each progenitor the gold clone keeps takes room a neighbour gives up.

36
00:02:26.527 --> 00:02:29.947
So the clone won by keeping more of its daughters as progenitors.

37
00:02:31.307 --> 00:02:32.307
Fate, not speed.

38
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.400
Now look down on the lining from above.

39
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.140
With time, gold patches spread among the blue.

40
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.220
The lining changed because which cells are there changed.

41
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.340
On average, the gold cells divided no faster.

42
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.790
Is a lining full of these clones dangerous?

43
00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.170
And how could anyone tell fate from speed in a living tissue?

44
00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.010
The text below picks up there.
